var e={id:`attacker`,label:`Attacker site`,sub:`evil.example`,x:15,y:22,tone:`attacker`},t={id:`victim`,label:`Victim`,sub:`signed in`,x:15,y:80,tone:`victim`},n={id:`browser`,label:`Victim's browser`,sub:`trusts cookies`,x:50,y:51,tone:`browser`},r={id:`target`,label:`Target site`,sub:`vault.example`,x:85,y:51,tone:`target`},i=[{id:`browser-loves-cookies`,title:`Why your browser sends cookies for you`,subtitle:`The automatic attachment that makes CSRF possible.`,category:`Foundations`,level:`beginner`,summary:`Before tokens or SameSite make sense, you have to see the one behaviour behind every CSRF bug: the browser lightly attaches your cookies to requests — without being asked.`,keyPoints:[`Cookies are attached by the browser, not by the page.`,`The server identifies you by the cookie alone.`,`Any site can cause the request; only your browser decides to attach the cookie.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`It starts with trust`,duration:9,caption:`Every time you visit a site you are signed into, your browser quietly proves who you are. It does this with a cookie.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Foundations`,title:`Why your browser sends cookies for you`,subtitle:`The automatic attachment behind CSRF.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`What a cookie is`,duration:11,caption:`A cookie is a small piece of text the site stored in your browser earlier. One of them usually holds your session.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`A cookie is just text`,items:[`The server sets it once, with Set-Cookie.`,`The browser stores it for that site.`,`It usually identifies your logged-in session.`]}},{id:`s3`,title:`The browser attaches it`,duration:13,caption:`When a request goes to that site, your browser adds the cookie automatically. You never see it, and the page never has to ask.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[t,n,r],packets:[{from:`victim`,to:`browser`,label:`opens vault.example`,at:.05,tone:`request`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`GET /account`,at:.4,tone:`request`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`+ session cookie`,at:.62,tone:`cookie`}],note:`The cookie rides along automatically — this is the whole lever.`}},{id:`s4`,title:`What the server sees`,duration:11,caption:`On the wire, the request simply carries a Cookie header. To the server, that is proof you are signed in.`,visual:{kind:`code`,lang:`http`,title:`The request the server sees`,code:`GET /account HTTP/1.1
Host: vault.example
Cookie: session=8f3a9c2e7b41

=> "Welcome back, Amina"`}},{id:`s4b`,title:`The automated weather app`,duration:10,caption:`Imagine a popular bookmarking site that always attaches your cookies to its own requests. The browser does the same, silently.`,visual:{kind:`browser`,url:`https://vault.example/account`,title:`Signed in as amina`,lines:[{type:`text`,value:`Welcome back, amina`},{type:`field`,label:`Email`,value:`amina@vault.example`},{type:`note`,value:`session cookie sent automatically`}]}},{id:`s4b2`,title:`One rule, everywhere`,duration:10,caption:`That automatic behaviour applies to every request to that site — including requests another website caused.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`The browser does not ask why`,items:[`It attaches cookies based on the destination.`,`It does not check who triggered the request.`,`That is exactly what an attacker exploits.`]}},{id:`s5`,title:`Another site can trigger it`,duration:13,caption:`Now imagine an attacker page causes your browser to request vault.example. The cookie still rides along, because the browser only looks at the destination.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[e,t,n,r],packets:[{from:`browser`,to:`attacker`,label:`loads evil.example`,at:.08,tone:`forged`},{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`hidden request`,at:.35,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`GET /account + cookie`,at:.62,tone:`cookie`}],note:`The victim never touched the target site — yet the cookie was sent.`}},{id:`s6`,title:`Why this matters`,duration:12,caption:`If that request changes something — an email, a password, a payment — the change happens as you. That is Cross-Site Request Forgery.`,visual:{kind:`impact`,heading:`A request you never made`,before:`email: amina@vault.example`,after:`email: attacker@evil.example`,note:`No password, no token — just your cookie.`}},{id:`s7`,title:`The takeaway`,duration:11,caption:`The browser attaches cookies automatically and forgets who asked. Every CSRF defence exists to add back the missing proof of intent.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Remember`,items:[`Cookies are attached by the browser, for the destination.`,`The server trusts the cookie as proof of you.`,`The fix is to prove the request came from your own page.`]}}]},{id:`confused-deputy`,title:`The confused deputy`,subtitle:`Why the browser happily helps the attacker.`,category:`Foundations`,level:`beginner`,summary:`Security people call the browser a “confused deputy”: it holds your authority (your cookies) but cannot tell a good request from a bad one. This is the mental model for all CSRF.`,keyPoints:[`A deputy is something that acts on your behalf.`,`The browser is confused because it obeys any page, not just yours.`,`CSRF is not a browser bug — it is a missing check on the server.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`A deputy with your keys`,duration:10,caption:`Think of your browser as a deputy that carries your keys — your cookies — and unlocks doors for whoever asks.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Foundations`,title:`The confused deputy`,subtitle:`Your browser holds your authority.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`What a deputy is`,duration:11,caption:`A deputy is anything that acts on your behalf. That is useful — until it cannot tell friend from stranger.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`A deputy in one line`,items:[`It holds your authority.`,`It acts when someone asks.`,`It is “confused” when it cannot tell who should be asking.`]}},{id:`s3`,title:`Two sites, one deputy`,duration:14,caption:`Here is the trap. The attacker page and your bank both talk to the same deputy. The deputy carries your bank key for both.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[e,t,n,r],packets:[{from:`victim`,to:`browser`,label:`browsing normally`,at:.05,tone:`request`},{from:`browser`,to:`attacker`,label:`visits evil.example`,at:.3,tone:`forged`},{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`asks for a favour`,at:.5,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`unlocks the bank`,at:.7,tone:`cookie`}],note:`The deputy never asks why the bank door needs opening.`}},{id:`s4`,title:`Confusion, not malice`,duration:11,caption:`The deputy is not evil. It simply lacks a way to check intent, so it does exactly what it is told.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Why “confused”`,items:[`It cannot see which page asked.`,`It only knows the destination.`,`So it applies your authority anyway.`]}},{id:`s5`,title:`What the attacker asks for`,duration:12,caption:`The favour is a request to the target site — maybe a hidden form that changes your email the moment the page loads.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`the favour`,lang:`html`,code:`<form action="https://vault.example/account/email"
      method="POST">
  <input type="hidden" name="email"
         value="attacker@evil.example">
</form>
<script>document.forms[0].submit()<\/script>`}},{id:`s6`,title:`The door opens`,duration:12,caption:`Because the cookie came along, the bank sees a fully authorised request. The email changes, and the deputy is none the wiser.`,visual:{kind:`impact`,heading:`Authorised… by mistake`,before:`email: amina@vault.example`,after:`email: attacker@evil.example`,note:`The server trusted the cookie, not the intent.`}},{id:`s7`,title:`The real fix`,duration:12,caption:`You cannot un-confuse the browser. So the server must demand proof that the request came from its own pages.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Where the fix belongs`,items:[`Not in the browser — it is doing its job.`,`In the server, by checking a token or the Origin.`,`Every state-changing action needs that proof.`]}}]},{id:`get-vs-post`,title:`GET, POST and unsafe reads`,subtitle:`Why changing state on GET invites CSRF.`,category:`Foundations`,level:`beginner`,summary:`A single design rule kills a whole class of CSRF bugs: use GET only to read, and POST to change. Here is exactly why an unsafe GET is so easy to trigger.`,keyPoints:[`GET should be safe: it must not change anything.`,`An <img> or <link> fires a GET with no click at all.`,`Changing state on GET turns a harmless tag into an attack.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`Two verbs, two jobs`,duration:10,caption:`HTTP gives us verbs. GET is meant to read. POST is meant to change. Mixing them up is where trouble starts.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Foundations`,title:`GET, POST and unsafe reads`,subtitle:`The rule that removes a whole class of bugs.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`Safe vs unsafe`,duration:12,caption:`A safe request only reads — it changes nothing. An unsafe request writes. The verb should tell you which one you are doing.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`The contract`,items:[`GET and HEAD: safe, read-only.`,`POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE: unsafe, they change state.`,`A GET that writes breaks the contract.`]}},{id:`s3`,title:`What a GET looks like`,duration:10,caption:`A GET puts everything in the URL, which is why images and links can carry one without any JavaScript.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`a normal GET`,lang:`http`,code:`GET /search?q=csrf HTTP/1.1
Host: shop.example
Cookie: session=abc123`}},{id:`s4`,title:`What a POST looks like`,duration:10,caption:`A POST carries its data in the body, usually from a form. It is the right verb for changing things.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`a normal POST`,lang:`http`,code:`POST /account/email HTTP/1.1
Host: vault.example
Cookie: session=abc123

email=amina@vault.example`}},{id:`s5`,title:`The dangerous GET`,duration:13,caption:`Now suppose the site changes your email with a GET: /account/email?value=… An attacker only needs you to load an image with that URL.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`attacker page`,lang:`html`,code:`<!-- no click, no script needed -->
<img src="https://vault.example/account/email
     ?value=attacker@evil.example">`}},{id:`s6`,title:`It fires by itself`,duration:13,caption:`The moment the page loads, the browser fetches the image. The GET runs, the cookie rides along, and the email changes.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[e,t,n,r],packets:[{from:`browser`,to:`attacker`,label:`loads page`,at:.06,tone:`forged`},{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`<img src=…>`,at:.32,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`GET changes email`,at:.6,tone:`cookie`}],note:`An image tag is enough. No form, no script.`}},{id:`s7`,title:`The rule`,duration:12,caption:`Keep GET honest: reading only. Every write goes through POST, and then most naive triggers simply stop working.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`The rule`,items:[`Read on GET. Write on POST.`,`Never change state from a URL alone.`,`Then add a token so POST is safe too.`]}}]},{id:`same-origin-plainly`,title:`The same-origin policy, plainly`,subtitle:`What it blocks — and the huge gap it leaves.`,category:`Foundations`,level:`beginner`,summary:`People assume the same-origin policy protects them from CSRF. It does not. Here is precisely what it stops, and what it never even tries to stop.`,keyPoints:[`An origin is scheme + host + port.`,`The policy blocks reading cross-site responses.`,`It does not block sending cross-site requests — the CSRF gap.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`The guard of the web`,duration:10,caption:`The same-origin policy is the rule that keeps one website from reading another website’s data. It is essential — but it has a blind spot.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Foundations`,title:`The same-origin policy`,subtitle:`What it stops — and what it does not.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`What counts as an origin`,duration:12,caption:`An origin is three things together: the scheme, the host, and the port. Change any one and it is a different origin.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Origin = scheme + host + port`,items:[`https://vault.example is one origin.`,`http://vault.example is a different origin.`,`https://evil.example is different again.`]}},{id:`s3`,title:`What it blocks`,duration:12,caption:`Script running on evil.example cannot read the response from vault.example. That stops a lot of quiet data theft.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[e,n,r],packets:[{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`fetch bank data`,at:.1,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`request sent`,at:.4,tone:`request`},{from:`target`,to:`browser`,label:`response`,at:.6,tone:`good`},{from:`browser`,to:`attacker`,label:`blocked by SOP`,at:.8,tone:`blocked`}],note:`Cross-site reads are refused.`}},{id:`s4`,title:`What it does not block`,duration:13,caption:`But the policy never said the request could not be sent. It only guards the response. That single gap is CSRF.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`The blind spot`,items:[`Sending a cross-site request is allowed.`,`The request still carries your cookies.`,`Only reading the reply is blocked.`]}},{id:`s5`,title:`Send yes, read no`,duration:13,caption:`An attacker does not need to read anything. If the request changes state, the damage is already done.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[e,n,r],packets:[{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`POST /transfer + cookie`,at:.4,tone:`cookie`},{from:`target`,to:`browser`,label:`response (unread)`,at:.7,tone:`blocked`}],note:`A CSRF attack is often blind — and still devastating.`}},{id:`s6`,title:`So who checks intent?`,duration:12,caption:`With the browser only guarding responses, the server must guard the request. That is the job of CSRF tokens and Origin checks.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`The division of labour`,items:[`Browser: stops cross-site reads.`,`Server: must verify request intent.`,`Never assume the browser did it for you.`]}},{id:`s7`,title:`The takeaway`,duration:10,caption:`The same-origin policy is a reader-guard, not a sender-guard. CSRF lives entirely in that gap.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Remember`,items:[`SOP blocks reading, not sending.`,`Cookies ride along with cross-site sends.`,`Prove intent on the server, every time.`]}}]}],a=[{id:`get-image-csrf`,title:`GET CSRF: the image tag`,subtitle:`The smallest possible attack.`,category:`Attacks`,level:`beginner`,accent:`danger`,summary:`If a site changes state on a GET, an attacker needs only an <img> tag. There is no click, no script, and nothing to notice — the request fires the instant the page loads.`,keyPoints:[`Image, script and link tags all issue GET requests.`,`The browser sends cookies with them automatically.`,`State-changing GETs are the easiest CSRF to trigger.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`The one-line attack`,duration:10,caption:`Some CSRF attacks are a single line of HTML. Today we build the smallest one: an image that changes your account.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Attacks`,title:`GET CSRF: the image tag`,subtitle:`No click. No script. Just a picture.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`The vulnerable endpoint`,duration:12,caption:`Imagine a site that changes your email with a GET, taking the new value straight from the URL.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`vulnerable target`,lang:`http`,code:`GET /account/email?value=amina@vault.example
Host: vault.example
Cookie: session=abc123`}},{id:`s3`,title:`Image tags fetch anything`,duration:12,caption:`An image tag does not know it is pointing at an API. It just fetches the URL, and the browser attaches your cookie.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`attacker page`,lang:`html`,code:`<img src="https://vault.example/account/email
     ?value=attacker@evil.example"
     width="1" height="1" alt="">`}},{id:`s4`,title:`It fires on load`,duration:13,caption:`The moment the victim opens the attacker page, the browser fetches that image — and the GET runs.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[e,t,n,r],packets:[{from:`browser`,to:`attacker`,label:`load page`,at:.06,tone:`forged`},{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`<img src=…>`,at:.34,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`GET + cookie`,at:.62,tone:`cookie`}],note:`The response is an image error — and it does not matter.`}},{id:`s5`,title:`The result`,duration:11,caption:`The server saw an authenticated request and changed the email. The victim saw nothing at all.`,visual:{kind:`impact`,heading:`Changed while you read the news`,before:`email: amina@vault.example`,after:`email: attacker@evil.example`,note:`One image tag did this.`}},{id:`s6`,title:`Other tags work too`,duration:12,caption:`It does not have to be an image. A stylesheet, a script, a video poster — anything that fetches a URL will do.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Several ways to fire a GET`,items:[`<img src=…> — the classic.`,`<link rel="stylesheet" href=…>`,`<script src=…> and <iframe src=…>.`]}},{id:`s6b`,title:`Even a redirect`,duration:11,caption:`You can also just navigate the victim straight to the URL, or hide it behind a redirect from the attacker site.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`also works`,lang:`http`,code:`HTTP/1.1 302 Found
Location: https://vault.example/account/email
          ?value=attacker@evil.example`}},{id:`s7`,title:`Seeing it clearly`,duration:10,caption:`To the victim, nothing happened. The browser never prompts, and the change is silent.`,visual:{kind:`browser`,url:`https://news.example/recipe`,title:`A harmless page`,lines:[{type:`text`,value:`Today: the perfect sponge cake`},{type:`note`,value:`1 hidden request left this page`}]}},{id:`s7b`,title:`Why it works`,duration:11,caption:`It works for one reason: a read-only verb was used to write. Move that write to POST and the image tag stops working.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`The single root cause`,items:[`The verb was GET, which any tag can send.`,`The cookie proved identity, not intent.`,`The server changed state without a token check.`]}},{id:`s8`,title:`The takeaway`,duration:11,caption:`Never change state on GET. When you do, an image is a weapon, and the browser hands it the ammunition.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Remember`,items:[`Read on GET, write on POST.`,`Any tag can fire a GET with cookies.`,`Add a token so even POST is safe.`]}}]},{id:`post-form-csrf`,title:`POST CSRF: the auto-submitting form`,subtitle:`When a token is missing, a form is enough.`,category:`Attacks`,level:`beginner`,accent:`danger`,summary:`Moving to POST is necessary but not sufficient. An attacker can build a form on their own site and submit it to yours; without a CSRF token, the change goes through exactly the same.`,keyPoints:[`Forms can post cross-site to any URL.`,`JavaScript can submit the form with no click.`,`POST alone is not a defence — a token is required.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`After you switch to POST`,duration:11,caption:`Good news: the site now changes email with POST, so image tags no longer work. But the attacker simply builds a form.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Attacks`,title:`POST CSRF: the auto-submitting form`,subtitle:`POST is not a defence by itself.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`Forms cross origins freely`,duration:12,caption:`A form on evil.example can post to vault.example. The browser allows cross-site form submissions by design.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`What a form can do`,items:[`Send a POST to any HTTPS URL.`,`Carry the victim’s cookies automatically.`,`Be submitted by the page itself.`]}},{id:`s3`,title:`The forged form`,duration:13,caption:`The attacker hides the real value and submits the form as soon as the page loads. The victim never sees a button.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`attacker page`,lang:`html`,code:`<form method="POST"
      action="https://vault.example/account/email">
  <input type="hidden" name="email"
         value="attacker@evil.example">
</form>
<script>document.forms[0].submit()<\/script>`}},{id:`s4`,title:`The trailer`,duration:13,caption:`Because the POST carries the session cookie, the server treats it as you, and the email changes immediately.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[e,n,r],packets:[{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`auto-submit form`,at:.12,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`POST + cookie`,at:.5,tone:`cookie`},{from:`target`,to:`browser`,label:`302 redirect`,at:.8,tone:`good`}],note:`The redirect is hidden by the attacker.`}},{id:`s5`,title:`No token, no proof`,duration:12,caption:`The server had one job: check for a secret token that only its own pages can produce. It did not, so the request slipped through.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`What was missing`,items:[`No hidden anti-CSRF token in the form.`,`No Origin or Referer check.`,`Only the cookie — which the attacker borrowed.`]}},{id:`s6`,title:`Variations`,duration:12,caption:`Attackers rarely stop at one technique. They add a token-shaped field, or post from a hidden iframe to look more legitimate.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Common tricks`,items:[`Fake csrf_token field filled with junk.`,`A hidden iframe to keep the page looking clean.`,`enctype tricks to dodge weak parsers.`]}},{id:`s6b`,title:`It does not need AJAX`,duration:11,caption:`There is no cross-origin scripting here, so CORS never even gets a say. This is plain old HTML doing the damage.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`No scripting required`,items:[`Forms are HTML, not script.`,`No CORS preflight for a simple form POST.`,`The browser does the work.`]}},{id:`s7`,title:`The result`,duration:11,caption:`Once again, the account email is the attacker’s. POST moved the goalposts; it did not build the wall.`,visual:{kind:`impact`,heading:`Still changed`,before:`email: amina@vault.example`,after:`email: attacker@evil.example`,note:`The token is the wall.`}},{id:`s8`,title:`The takeaway`,duration:11,caption:`Use POST for writes — then go one step further and require a per-session token the attacker cannot guess or copy.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Remember`,items:[`POST raises the bar, but is not enough.`,`A same-origin token proves intent.`,`Verify it on every state change.`]}}]},{id:`login-csrf`,title:`Login CSRF: when the attacker logs you in`,subtitle:`The attack that makes you the intruder.`,category:`Attacks`,level:`intermediate`,accent:`danger`,summary:`Login CSRF flips the usual story: instead of acting as you, the attacker makes your browser log into their account. Everything you do next lands in their world, and they can read it.`,keyPoints:[`The forged request is the login form, not a state change on your account.`,`You end up signed into the attacker’s account unknowingly.`,`Anything you save or search is now visible to the attacker.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`A surprising twist`,duration:11,caption:`So far the attacker acted as you. Login CSRF does the opposite: it signs you into the attacker’s account without asking.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Attacks`,title:`Login CSRF`,subtitle:`You become the intruder.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`The forged login`,duration:13,caption:`The attacker posts their own credentials to the target’s login form from their page. Your browser sends the request for them.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`attacker page`,lang:`html`,code:`<form method="POST" action="https://shop.example/login">
  <input type="hidden" name="user" value="mallory">
  <input type="hidden" name="pass" value="mallory-pw">
</form>
<script>document.forms[0].submit()<\/script>`}},{id:`s3`,title:`You are now Mallory`,duration:13,caption:`The server responds with a new session cookie — for the attacker’s account. From now on, your browsing happens inside their account.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[e,n,r],packets:[{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`auto-login form`,at:.12,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`POST /login`,at:.5,tone:`request`},{from:`target`,to:`browser`,label:`Set-Cookie: mallory`,at:.8,tone:`cookie`}],note:`The victim is signed in as someone else.`}},{id:`s4`,title:`What you do next`,duration:12,caption:`You might paste a private note, add a delivery address, or search for something sensitive — all thinking it is your own account.`,visual:{kind:`browser`,url:`https://shop.example/account`,title:`Signed in — as someone else`,lines:[{type:`text`,value:`Welcome, Mallory`},{type:`note`,value:`the victim sees no warning`},{type:`field`,label:`Saved address`,value:`12 Rose Lane`}]}},{id:`s4b`,title:`A shopping trap`,duration:12,caption:`On a shop, anything you purchase is billed to the attacker, or your saved details become visible to them. It is your data, their account.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`What leaks`,items:[`Search history and saved addresses.`,`Uploads and notes you add.`,`Card details you enter next time.`]}},{id:`s5`,title:`The attacker reads later`,duration:12,caption:`The attacker simply logs in as themselves and inspects everything the victim left behind. No exploit — just collected data.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[e,r],packets:[{from:`attacker`,to:`target`,label:`login as mallory`,at:.3,tone:`request`}],note:`Everything you saved is now theirs to read.`}},{id:`s5b`,title:`Why it is dangerous`,duration:11,caption:`It is subtle. Nothing looks broken, the site works normally, and the victim has no reason to suspect a thing.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`The danger`,items:[`No visible error or prompt.`,`The victim keeps using the site normally.`,`The attacker gains a stream of personal data.`]}},{id:`s6`,title:`Result`,duration:10,caption:`The attacker never needed your password. They only needed you to log in for them.`,visual:{kind:`impact`,heading:`Identity swapped`,before:`your searches, your data`,after:`poured into the attacker’s account`,note:`Silent, and easy to miss.`}},{id:`s7`,title:`The takeaway`,duration:11,caption:`Logins need CSRF protection too. A token on the login form stops the attacker from making you sign in as them.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Remember`,items:[`Login and signup are state changes.`,`Protect them with a token like any other.`,`Consider re-authentication for sensitive accounts.`]}}]},{id:`logout-csrf`,title:`Logout CSRF and quiet nuisances`,subtitle:`Small attacks that add up.`,category:`Attacks`,level:`beginner`,accent:`steel`,summary:`Not every CSRF attack steals something. Some just log you out, change a setting, or cause confusion. Individually minor, together they enable bigger attacks.`,keyPoints:[`Logout is a state change and can be CSRF’d.`,`Forced logout can be chained with other bugs.`,`Even “harmless” changes deserve a token check.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`The forgotten endpoint`,duration:10,caption:`Teams protect the important actions and forget the little ones. Logout is a favourite that gets missed.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Attacks`,title:`Logout CSRF`,subtitle:`Small, but never truly harmless.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`A logout link`,duration:12,caption:`If logout is a GET, an attacker can embed it anywhere. The victim is silently signed out on the next page load.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`attacker page`,lang:`html`,code:`<img src="https://bank.example/logout">
<img src="https://bank.example/logout">`}},{id:`s3`,title:`Why annoy someone?`,duration:12,caption:`On its own, being logged out is just annoying. But it is a piece of a larger puzzle an attacker can use.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Why it matters`,items:[`It forces re-authentication flows.`,`It can push users onto weaker recovery paths.`,`It can be chained with session-fixation tricks.`]}},{id:`s4`,title:`Forced logout chain`,duration:13,caption:`Log the victim out, then lure them to a fake login page. Confused, they re-enter credentials on the attacker’s form.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[e,t,r],packets:[{from:`attacker`,to:`victim`,label:`logout + fake login`,at:.2,tone:`forged`},{from:`victim`,to:`attacker`,label:`re-enters password`,at:.6,tone:`cookie`}],note:`CSRF becomes the first step of credential phishing.`}},{id:`s5`,title:`Other quiet changes`,duration:12,caption:`Logout is not the only minor one. Changing a language, a theme, or a notification setting are all state changes too.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Low-stakes, still state`,items:[`Change preferred language or currency.`,`Toggle notifications or privacy settings.`,`Add a hidden contact or forwarding rule.`]}},{id:`s5b`,title:`The sneaky one`,duration:12,caption:`Watch out for adding a mail forwarding rule. It looks harmless, but it quietly copies every message to the attacker.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`a “minor” setting`,lang:`http`,code:`POST /settings/forward HTTP/1.1
Host: mail.example

forward_to=attacker@evil.example`}},{id:`s6`,title:`Impact`,duration:11,caption:`Nothing seemed to break, yet a rule now leaks your mail, or a logout set up a phishing page. Quiet attacks are the dangerous ones.`,visual:{kind:`impact`,heading:`Unnoticed`,before:`normal looking session`,after:`forwarding rule active`,note:`The victim never suspects.`}},{id:`s7`,title:`The takeaway`,duration:11,caption:`Protect every state change, big or small. If it changes something, it needs a CSRF token and a safe verb.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Remember`,items:[`Logout and settings are state changes.`,`Small bugs chain into big ones.`,`One policy: token every write.`]}}]},{id:`json-api-csrf`,title:`JSON and API CSRF`,subtitle:`Why “it needs JSON” is not a defence.`,category:`Attacks`,level:`advanced`,accent:`danger`,summary:`Teams often say “our API only accepts JSON, so CSRF is impossible.” That is a myth. Here is how content-type assumptions break down, and why you still need real protection.`,keyPoints:[`A cross-site form cannot send application/json — but that is not the whole story.`,`Servers that ignore content-type or accept alternate encodings are exposed.`,`Never rely on content-type as your only CSRF defence.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`The comfortable claim`,duration:11,caption:`You will hear it often: “Our endpoints only accept JSON, and forms cannot send JSON, so we are safe.”`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Attacks`,title:`JSON and API CSRF`,subtitle:`Why “it needs JSON” is not a defence.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`The kernel of truth`,duration:12,caption:`It is true that an HTML form can only send three content types: urlencoded, multipart, and plain text.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`What a form can send`,items:[`application/x-www-form-urlencoded`,`multipart/form-data`,`text/plain`]}},{id:`s3`,title:`So far, so good`,duration:12,caption:`If your server truly rejects every other content type with a strict check, a plain form cannot reach it. That is a real, valid defence.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`strict API`,lang:`http`,code:`POST /api/email HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: text/plain

{"email":"attacker@evil.example"}

→ 415 Unsupported Media Type`}},{id:`s4`,title:`But assumptions break`,duration:13,caption:`Many frameworks parse a JSON body no matter the content type, or accept text/plain and then happily parse it as JSON.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`the leaky server`,lang:`http`,code:`POST /api/email HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: text/plain

{"email":"attacker@evil.example"}

→ 200 OK (parsed anyway)`}},{id:`s5`,title:`The text/plain form`,duration:13,caption:`To exploit the leaky server, the attacker uses a form with text/plain encoding. The body stays close enough to JSON to parse.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`attacker page`,lang:`html`,code:`<form method="POST" enctype="text/plain"
      action="https://api.example/email">
  <input name='{"email":"attacker@evil.example","x":"'
         value='"}>
</form>`}},{id:`s5b`,title:`Making it valid`,duration:12,caption:`The trailing equals sign a form adds is hidden inside the last JSON string, so the body still parses cleanly.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`The trick`,items:[`text/plain avoids a preflight.`,`The form’s extra “=” is smuggled into a string.`,`The server parses it as valid JSON.`]}},{id:`s6`,title:`Other doors`,duration:12,caption:`Some APIs accept method override headers, XML, or form-encoded bodies on the same endpoint. Each one reopens the door.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Common variants`,items:[`X-HTTP-Method-Override: POST → PUT`,`Form-encoded fallbacks on the same route`,`XML or JSONP content negotiation`]}},{id:`s7`,title:`Result`,duration:11,caption:`The API changed state from a cross-site form, cookies included. The content-type was never a wall, only a fence.`,visual:{kind:`impact`,heading:`API changed`,before:`email: amina@vault.example`,after:`email: attacker@evil.example`,note:`No preflight, no token, no check.`}},{id:`s8`,title:`The takeaway`,duration:12,caption:`Treat content-type as a hint, never a guarantee. A CSRF token or an Origin check protects the API the same way it protects a form.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Remember`,items:[`Enforce a strict content type AND a token.`,`Check Origin on API writes.`,`Never rely on JSON alone.`]}}]}],o=[{id:`token-synchronizer`,title:`Anti-CSRF tokens: the synchronizer pattern`,subtitle:`The industry-standard fix, step by step.`,category:`Defenses`,level:`intermediate`,accent:`ember`,summary:`The synchronizer token pattern is the gold standard. The server puts a secret, unpredictable token in its own forms, and rejects any state change that arrives without the matching token.`,keyPoints:[`The token is bound to the user’s session.`,`It is embedded in same-origin pages only.`,`The attacker cannot read it, so they cannot include it.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`Proving intent`,duration:10,caption:`A cookie proves who you are. A CSRF token proves the request came from your own page. Together they close the gap.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Defences`,title:`Anti-CSRF tokens`,subtitle:`The synchronizer pattern.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`Issue a secret`,duration:12,caption:`When the session starts, the server generates a random, unguessable token and ties it to that session.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`The token`,items:[`Random and unpredictable.`,`Bound to the user’s session.`,`Secret — the attacker must never see it.`]}},{id:`s3`,title:`Embed it in the page`,duration:13,caption:`Every form the server renders includes the token in a hidden field. The browser sends it back with the POST.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`server-rendered form`,lang:`html`,code:`<form method="POST" action="/account/email">
  <input type="hidden" name="csrf_token"
         value="a1b2c3d4e5f6">
  <input name="email" value="amina@vault.example">
  <button>Save</button>
</form>`}},{id:`s4`,title:`Verify every write`,duration:13,caption:`On submit, the server compares the token in the request with the one stored in the session. No match, no change.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[n,r],packets:[{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`POST + cookie + token`,at:.3,tone:`request`},{from:`target`,to:`browser`,label:`token matches → 200`,at:.7,tone:`good`}],note:`Cookie says who. Token says “from our page”.`}},{id:`s5`,title:`Why the attacker cannot copy it`,duration:13,caption:`The attacker can make the victim’s browser send the cookie, but the same-origin policy stops them from reading any page that contains the token.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[e,n,r],packets:[{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`tries to read page`,at:.15,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`fetch token`,at:.45,tone:`request`},{from:`browser`,to:`attacker`,label:`blocked by SOP`,at:.8,tone:`blocked`}],note:`No token → the forged request is rejected.`}},{id:`s6`,title:`A forged request fails`,duration:12,caption:`When the attacker submits the form without the token, the server sees the mismatch and refuses.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`rejected`,lang:`http`,code:`POST /account/email HTTP/1.1
Cookie: session=abc123

email=attacker@evil.example
(no csrf_token)

→ 403 Forbidden`}},{id:`s7`,title:`For single-page apps`,duration:13,caption:`SPAs read the token from a cookie the JavaScript can access, then echo it back in a header. Same idea, different transport.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`SPA pattern`,lang:`javascript`,code:`fetch("/api/email", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "X-CSRF-Token": readCookie("csrf") },
  body: JSON.stringify({ email })
})`}},{id:`s8`,title:`The rules`,duration:12,caption:`Keep it simple: generate per session, verify on every state-changing request, and never expose it to other origins.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Do it right`,items:[`One token per session, kept secret.`,`Check it on all POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE.`,`Reuse it, do not invent per-request complexity.`]}},{id:`s9`,title:`The takeaway`,duration:11,caption:`The synchronizer token is the gold standard because it makes intent explicit. The attacker can cause a request, but cannot supply the proof.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Remember`,items:[`Tokens prove the request came from your page.`,`They are secret thanks to the same-origin policy.`,`Verify before every state change.`]}}]},{id:`double-submit`,title:`Double-submit cookies`,subtitle:`A stateless token that rides in two places.`,category:`Defences`,level:`intermediate`,accent:`steel`,summary:`Double-submit works without server-side session storage: the token is stored in a cookie and echoed back in a header, and the server only needs to confirm the two match.`,keyPoints:[`The token lives in a readable cookie and a request header.`,`The server only compares the two values.`,`Subdomains and cookie injection make it trickier than it looks.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`Token without a store`,duration:11,caption:`The synchronizer token needs the server to remember it. Double-submit avoids that: it trusts a cookie the JavaScript can read.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Defences`,title:`Double-submit cookies`,subtitle:`A stateless CSRF token.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`Two copies`,duration:12,caption:`The server sets a random token in a cookie. Page JavaScript reads it and copies it into a request header.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`both places`,lang:`javascript`,code:`// cookie: Set-Cookie: csrf=xyz; SameSite=Lax
// header added by the page:
headers["X-CSRF-Token"] = readCookie("csrf")`}},{id:`s3`,title:`The comparison`,duration:12,caption:`On each request the server checks that the cookie and the header match. That is the entire check.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`server check`,lang:`javascript`,code:`if (req.cookies.csrf !== req.headers["x-csrf-token"]) {
  return res.status(403).end()
}`}},{id:`s4`,title:`Why it works`,duration:13,caption:`An attacker can cause the cookie to be sent, but cannot read it to put the same value in the header of a forged request.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[e,n,r],packets:[{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`cookie: csrf=xyz`,at:.3,tone:`cookie`},{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`read csrf?`,at:.55,tone:`blocked`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`no matching header`,at:.8,tone:`blocked`}],note:`Matching requires reading — which cross-site reads cannot do.`}},{id:`s5`,title:`The catch: subdomains`,duration:13,caption:`A writable subdomain can overwrite the cookie. If shop.example lets attackers set cookies on the parent domain, double-submit breaks.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Where it gets fragile`,items:[`A subdomain can set a cookie on the parent domain.`,`Then it controls both copies.`,`Use a __Host- prefix to lock the cookie down.`]}},{id:`s5b`,title:`The stronger variant`,duration:12,caption:`Harden it by binding the token to the session and using the __Host- cookie prefix, which forbids domain and path edits.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`hardened cookie`,lang:`http`,code:`Set-Cookie: __Host-csrf=xyz; Path=/; Secure;
            SameSite=Lax; HttpOnly=false`}},{id:`s6`,title:`When to use it`,duration:12,caption:`Reach for double-submit when you cannot store state on the server, or when you want the simplest possible check across services.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Good fit`,items:[`Stateless or distributed backends.`,`Multiple services behind one domain.`,`When you also add SameSite for defence in depth.`]}},{id:`s7`,title:`The takeaway`,duration:11,caption:`Double-submit is light and effective, provided you lock down cookies. Its weakness is always the cookie scope, never the idea.`,visual:{kind:`impact`,heading:`The idea vs the details`,before:`match cookie and header`,after:`__Host- prefix + SameSite`,note:`The details make or break it.`}}]},{id:`samesite-deep-dive`,title:`SameSite: Lax, Strict and None`,subtitle:`The browser’s built-in first line of defence.`,category:`Defences`,level:`intermediate`,accent:`ember`,summary:`SameSite tells the browser when to attach a cookie to cross-site requests. Getting Lax, Strict and None right removes a large share of CSRF risk before your code even runs.`,keyPoints:[`Strict: never sent cross-site. Lax: sent on top-level GET navigation. None: always sent.`,`Modern browsers default to Lax when unset.`,`SameSite is strong, but should be defence in depth — not the only layer.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`A cookie setting with power`,duration:11,caption:`SameSite is an attribute on a cookie that tells the browser which cross-site requests may carry it. It is your first line of defence.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Defences`,title:`SameSite: Lax, Strict, None`,subtitle:`Control when cookies travel.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`Strict`,duration:12,caption:`Strict means the cookie is only sent for same-site requests. Nothing from evil.example will ever carry it.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`SameSite=Strict`,items:[`Never sent on any cross-site request.`,`Strongest protection.`,`A link from another site lands you logged out until you reload.`]}},{id:`s3`,title:`Lax`,duration:12,caption:`Lax is the common balance. It sends the cookie on top-level GET navigations, like clicking a link, but not on cross-site form posts.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`SameSite=Lax`,items:[`Sent on top-level GET navigation.`,`Blocked on cross-site POST and subrequests.`,`Blocks most CSRF while keeping normal links working.`]}},{id:`s4`,title:`None`,duration:12,caption:`None means always send the cookie, even cross-site. It requires Secure, and it gives you no CSRF protection at all.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`SameSite=None`,items:[`Cookie always attached.`,`Must be paired with Secure.`,`Use it only for genuine cross-site embeds, with tokens alongside.`]}},{id:`s5`,title:`The default changed`,duration:12,caption:`Browsers now treat a cookie with no SameSite as Lax. That quietly protects many old sites that never set it.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`modern default`,lang:`http`,code:`Set-Cookie: session=abc123

// modern browsers treat this as SameSite=Lax`}},{id:`s6`,title:`How it stops a forged POST`,duration:13,caption:`A cross-site form post under Lax simply does not carry the cookie, so the server sees an anonymous request and does nothing.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[e,n,r],packets:[{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`auto-submit POST`,at:.2,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`POST (no cookie)`,at:.6,tone:`blocked`}],note:`No cookie → no session → no change.`}},{id:`s7`,title:`Where it is not enough`,duration:12,caption:`Lax still allows top-level GETs. If a sensitive action is reachable by GET, SameSite will happily let that request through.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Know the limits`,items:[`Top-level GET navigations still carry the cookie.`,`Old or unusual browsers may ignore it.`,`Subdomain cookie tricks can weaken it.`]}},{id:`s8`,title:`The takeaway`,duration:11,caption:`Set SameSite deliberately: Strict for sensitive cookies, Lax as a safe default. Then keep a token as your second line.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Remember`,items:[`Lax is a strong, sensible default.`,`Strict for the most sensitive cookies.`,`Defence in depth: pair it with tokens.`]}}]},{id:`origin-referer`,title:`Origin and Referer checks`,subtitle:`Trust the header that names the sender.`,category:`Defences`,level:`intermediate`,accent:`steel`,summary:`Every request carries clues about where it came from. Checking the Origin header, with Referer as a fallback, is a simple and powerful CSRF defence — if you handle the edge cases.`,keyPoints:[`Origin tells you the source origin of a request.`,`Referer is a fallback but can be stripped or trimmed.`,`Reject unknown or missing values for state-changing requests.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`Ask where it came from`,duration:10,caption:`Instead of a token, the server can simply ask: which site sent this request? The Origin header answers that.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Defences`,title:`Origin and Referer checks`,subtitle:`Validate the sender.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`The Origin header`,duration:12,caption:`On cross-site requests, the browser adds an Origin header naming the site that caused the request.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`a cross-site POST`,lang:`http`,code:`POST /account/email HTTP/1.1
Host: vault.example
Origin: https://evil.example
Cookie: session=abc123`}},{id:`s3`,title:`The simple check`,duration:12,caption:`The server compares Origin with its own. If it does not match the expected site, the request is rejected.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`server rule`,lang:`javascript`,code:`const ok = req.headers.origin === "https://vault.example"
if (!ok) return res.status(403).end()`}},{id:`s4`,title:`Why an attacker cannot fake it`,duration:13,caption:`The page in a browser cannot set Origin freely; the browser controls it. That is what makes the header trustworthy.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Set by the browser`,items:[`Scripts cannot choose the Origin value.`,`It reflects the actual source origin.`,`A forged request carries the attacker’s origin.`]}},{id:`s5`,title:`Referer as backup`,duration:12,caption:`Older requests may not include Origin. Referer gives the full source URL, and you can check its origin part.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`fallback`,lang:`http`,code:`Referer: https://vault.example/settings
Origin: (sometimes absent)`}},{id:`s5b`,title:`The privacy wrinkle`,duration:12,caption:`Referer can be trimmed by policy or missing entirely. So treat a missing value carefully — usually you reject the write.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Handle with care`,items:[`Referrer-Policy may shorten or drop it.`,`Missing on some same-origin requests.`,`Reject unknown values on writes, or fall back to a token.`]}},{id:`s6`,title:`A forged request fails`,duration:12,caption:`The attacker’s cross-site form arrives with the wrong Origin, and the server turns it away immediately.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[e,n,r],packets:[{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`auto-submit POST`,at:.2,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`Origin: evil.example`,at:.55,tone:`blocked`},{from:`target`,to:`browser`,label:`403 Forbidden`,at:.85,tone:`good`}],note:`Wrong origin, request refused.`}},{id:`s7`,title:`The takeaway`,duration:11,caption:`Origin checks are cheap and effective. Combine them with tokens so neither one has to carry the whole burden.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Remember`,items:[`Check Origin on every state-changing request.`,`Use Referer only as a fallback.`,`No token means unknown/missing values must fail closed.`]}}]},{id:`custom-header-cors`,title:`Custom headers and CORS as defence`,subtitle:`Turning the preflight into a shield.`,category:`Defences`,level:`advanced`,accent:`ember`,summary:`Requiring a custom header forces a CORS preflight that cross-site forms cannot satisfy. Combined with a strict CORS policy, this turns a browser rule into an effective CSRF barrier.`,keyPoints:[`A custom header triggers a preflight the attacker cannot pass.`,`Strict CORS blocks the response and the request.`,`This works for APIs; use tokens where you can.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`Make the browser help`,duration:10,caption:`Sometimes the strongest defence is to require something a browser only lets your own site send.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Defences`,title:`Custom headers and CORS`,subtitle:`Turn browser rules into a shield.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`The trigger`,duration:12,caption:`A “simple” request avoids a preflight. Add a custom header, and the browser must ask permission first.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Simple vs preflighted`,items:[`Simple form posts cause no preflight.`,`A custom header like X-Requested-With forces one.`,`The attacker’s origin cannot pass the preflight.`]}},{id:`s3`,title:`Require the header`,duration:13,caption:`The API refuses any state-changing request that lacks your custom header. A cross-site form cannot add it.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`the rule`,lang:`javascript`,code:`if (req.headers["x-requested-with"] !== "fetch") {
  return res.status(403).end()
}`}},{id:`s4`,title:`The blocked preflight`,duration:13,caption:`The attacker’s page tries the request. The browser sends an OPTIONS preflight, and the strict CORS policy refuses it.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[e,n,r],packets:[{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`OPTIONS preflight`,at:.3,tone:`request`},{from:`target`,to:`browser`,label:`no CORS headers`,at:.6,tone:`blocked`},{from:`browser`,to:`attacker`,label:`request never sent`,at:.85,tone:`blocked`}],note:`No preflight approval → no request.`}},{id:`s5`,title:`Keep CORS strict`,duration:12,caption:`The defence only holds if CORS stays strict. Reflecting arbitrary origins, or allowing all with credentials, undoes it.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Do not undo it`,items:[`Never reflect any Origin back blindly.`,`Never combine a wildcard with credentials.`,`List your allowed origins explicitly.`]}},{id:`s5b`,title:`The dangerous combo`,duration:12,caption:`Reflecting the Origin and allowing credentials means the attacker’s page is trusted, and CSRF protection vanishes.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`a classic flaw`,lang:`http`,code:`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://evil.example
Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true

→ the attacker can read everything`}},{id:`s6`,title:`When to use it`,duration:12,caption:`This suits pure APIs used by a single front end. Keep it as defence in depth, and still require a token for sensitive actions.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Good fit`,items:[`JSON APIs with a known front end.`,`When you cannot set SameSite cookies.`,`Always alongside a CSRF token.`]}},{id:`s7`,title:`The takeaway`,duration:11,caption:`A required custom header forces a preflight the attacker cannot win. Strict CORS makes that a real barrier, not a formality.`,visual:{kind:`impact`,heading:`Browser rule as barrier`,before:`simple cross-site POST`,after:`blocked at the preflight`,note:`But keep CORS strict.`}}]}],s=[{id:`xss-beats-csrf`,title:`When XSS defeats CSRF`,subtitle:`Why script injection overrides every token.`,category:`Bypasses`,level:`advanced`,accent:`danger`,summary:`Every CSRF defence assumes the attacker cannot read your pages. Cross-site scripting breaks that assumption. Once code runs on your origin, it can read the token and forge perfect requests.`,keyPoints:[`Tokens stop cross-site requests, not same-site scripts.`,`XSS removes the same-origin boundary that protects the token.`,`The real fix is preventing XSS in the first place.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`The one thing tokens assume`,duration:11,caption:`Every CSRF token relies on a promise: that the attacker cannot read your page. Cross-site scripting makes that promise false.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Bypasses`,title:`When XSS defeats CSRF`,subtitle:`Script injection overrides tokens.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`Tokens vs cross-site`,duration:12,caption:`A CSRF token blocks a request from evil.example, because that page cannot read the token from your site.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[e,n,r],packets:[{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`cross-site POST`,at:.2,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`no valid token`,at:.6,tone:`blocked`}],note:`Blocked — the attacker never saw the token.`}},{id:`s3`,title:`Now inject a script`,duration:13,caption:`If the site has an XSS flaw, the attacker runs their JavaScript on vault.example itself. It is same-origin now.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`the injection`,lang:`html`,code:`https://vault.example/search?q=<script>
fetch("/account/email").then(r=>r.text())
<\/script>`}},{id:`s4`,title:`Reading the token`,duration:13,caption:`Same-origin script can read the page, including any CSRF token, because the same-origin policy no longer separates it from your data.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`same-origin read`,lang:`javascript`,code:`const page = await fetch("/settings").then(r=>r.text())
const token = page.match(/csrf_token" value="([^"]+)/)[1]
// the attacker now holds a valid token`}},{id:`s5`,title:`A perfect forged request`,duration:13,caption:`With the token in hand, the attacker sends a request that passes every CSRF check. Cookie, token, Origin — all correct.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[n,r],packets:[{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`POST + cookie + real token`,at:.3,tone:`cookie`},{from:`target`,to:`browser`,label:`200 OK — accepted`,at:.75,tone:`good`}],note:`CSRF defences cannot detect this — it is same-origin.`}},{id:`s5b`,title:`SameSite cannot save you`,duration:12,caption:`SameSite does not help either. The forged request is same-site, so the cookie is attached as normal.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Every layer fails`,items:[`SameSite: request is same-site.`,`Origin: the origin is genuinely yours.`,`Token: read directly from your page.`]}},{id:`s6`,title:`Result`,duration:11,caption:`The account is fully taken over, and no CSRF control raised an alarm, because none of them were designed for this.`,visual:{kind:`impact`,heading:`Full bypass`,before:`a valid token you cannot read`,after:`a valid token the attacker read`,note:`XSS wins.`}},{id:`s7`,title:`The takeaway`,duration:11,caption:`CSRF tokens are not a silver bullet. Prevent XSS with output encoding, and treat any XSS as a total compromise.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Remember`,items:[`Tokens only stop cross-site, not same-site, scripts.`,`XSS defeats every CSRF defence.`,`Fix the injection, not just the token.`]}}]},{id:`samesite-bypass`,title:`Bypassing SameSite`,subtitle:`The gaps Lax leaves open.`,category:`Bypasses`,level:`advanced`,accent:`danger`,summary:`SameSite is powerful, but it is not airtight. Lax still permits top-level GET navigations, and a few timing and subdomain tricks can reopen doors. Treat it as one layer, never the only one.`,keyPoints:[`Lax allows top-level GET navigations with cookies.`,`The “Lax + POST” grace window exists for newly set cookies.`,`Subdomain and open-redirect tricks can turn GETs into attacks.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`Powerful, not perfect`,duration:10,caption:`SameSite stopped a huge amount of CSRF overnight. But skilled attackers look for the gaps it leaves.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Bypasses`,title:`Bypassing SameSite`,subtitle:`The gaps Lax leaves open.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`Lax allows top-level GET`,duration:12,caption:`Under Lax, a top-level GET navigation still carries the cookie. If any sensitive action uses GET, that is an open door.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`What Lax still sends`,items:[`Cookies on top-level GET navigations.`,`So a GET that changes state is exposed.`,`This is why GET must never change state.`]}},{id:`s3`,title:`The two-minute window`,duration:13,caption:`A newly set cookie is exempt from Lax for a short grace period. An attacker who can force a fresh cookie can exploit that window.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`The “Lax + POST” grace`,items:[`Applies only to brand-new cookies.`,`Lasts around two minutes.`,`Can allow a cross-site POST right after login.`]}},{id:`s4`,title:`Method override`,duration:13,caption:`Some servers turn a GET into a state-changing action via a method override parameter. Lax hands them the cookie on the GET.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`GET that writes`,lang:`http`,code:`GET /account/email?_method=POST&value=attacker@evil.example
Cookie: session=abc123  (sent: top-level GET)`}},{id:`s5`,title:`Subdomain and redirect tricks`,duration:13,caption:`A writable subdomain can set cookies on the parent, and an open redirect on your own site can turn a cross-site link into a same-site navigation.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[e,n,r],packets:[{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`link to your redirect`,at:.2,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`redirect to sensitive URL`,at:.6,tone:`cookie`}],note:`An open redirect on your own origin becomes a pivot.`}},{id:`s5b`,title:`Cookie scope matters`,duration:12,caption:`A cookie set by a subdomain is a classic wedge. The __Host- prefix, which forbids domain scoping, closes it.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`lock it down`,lang:`http`,code:`Set-Cookie: __Host-session=abc; Path=/; Secure; SameSite=Lax

// cannot be set by a subdomain`}},{id:`s6`,title:`Impact`,duration:11,caption:`Each gap is small, but chained together they reopen the attack. SameSite alone is never the whole story.`,visual:{kind:`impact`,heading:`Defence weakened`,before:`SameSite=Lax only`,after:`token + strict CORS + safe methods`,note:`Layering closes the gaps.`}},{id:`s7`,title:`The takeaway`,duration:11,caption:`Keep Lax as your default, but add tokens, avoid GET writes, and lock down cookies with the __Host- prefix.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Remember`,items:[`Lax permits top-level GET with cookies.`,`New-cookie grace and subdomains are gaps.`,`Layer defences; never rely on one.`]}}]},{id:`weak-token-replay`,title:`Weak tokens and replay`,subtitle:`When the counter-measure is breakable.`,category:`Bypasses`,level:`advanced`,accent:`danger`,summary:`A CSRF token is only as good as its randomness and its lifecycle. Predictable tokens, tokens that never rotate, or tokens accepted from anywhere make the defence collapse quietly.`,keyPoints:[`Tokens must be unpredictable — no timestamps, no counters.`,`Tokens should be per-session and validated server-side.`,`Accepting a token from a cookie alone invites replay.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`A lock made of glass`,duration:11,caption:`A weak token is worse than no token, because it feels safe while being easy to crack.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Bypasses`,title:`Weak tokens and replay`,subtitle:`When the counter-measure is breakable.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`Predictable`,duration:12,caption:`If the token is a timestamp, a counter, or a simple hash of the user id, an attacker can simply guess it.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`predictable tokens`,lang:`text`,code:`csrf=1712345678        ← a timestamp
csrf=10042             # a counter
csrf=md5("user-42")    ← a derived value`}},{id:`s3`,title:`Predictable = forgeable`,duration:12,caption:`Once the pattern is known, the attacker generates a valid token themselves. The check passes, and the defence is gone.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Broken randomness`,items:[`Guessable values from time or counters.`,`Tokens derived from public data.`,`Short tokens brute-forced quickly.`]}},{id:`s4`,title:`Never rotating`,duration:12,caption:`A single token for the whole session means one leak compromises every future request. Tokens should shorten their useful life.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Lifecycle mistakes`,items:[`One token shared across every session.`,`A token that never expires.`,`A token that survives logout and back.`]}},{id:`s5`,title:`The replay trap`,duration:13,caption:`If the server accepts a token from a cookie alone, an attacker who can set a cookie can replay any value, and the check passes.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[e,n,r],packets:[{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`set cookie csrf=v`,at:.2,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`header + cookie = v`,at:.6,tone:`cookie`},{from:`target`,to:`browser`,label:`match → 200`,at:.85,tone:`good`}],note:`The check compared two values the attacker controlled.`}},{id:`s5b`,title:`Cookie injection again`,duration:12,caption:`This is why double-submit needs a __Host- cookie: without it, a subdomain or a cookie-fixing bug lets the attacker supply both halves.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Where replay starts`,items:[`A writable subdomain sets the cookie.`,`A cookie-tossing bug fixes the value.`,`Then both sides of the check agree.`]}},{id:`s6`,title:`Result`,duration:11,caption:`The site believes it has token protection. In reality the token is predictable, or the attacker has replayed it with a forged cookie.`,visual:{kind:`impact`,heading:`A false sense of safety`,before:`“we validate csrf_token”`,after:`a token the attacker guessed`,note:`Strength and lifecycle matter.`}},{id:`s7`,title:`The takeaway`,duration:11,caption:`Generate tokens with a secure random source, bind them to the session, rotate on login, and never trust a value the client can set.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Remember`,items:[`Use a CSPRNG for every token.`,`Bind to session; rotate on login.`,`Serve secrets only in the page, not in cookies.`]}}]},{id:`method-override`,title:`Method override and content-type confusion`,subtitle:`When the framework reopens the door.`,category:`Bypasses`,level:`advanced`,accent:`danger`,summary:`Many frameworks let a GET or a form-encoded request behave like a PUT or a JSON call. Attackers abuse these conveniences to reach endpoints that thought they were protected.`,keyPoints:[`Method override headers turn safe verbs into unsafe ones.`,`Content-type negotiation can accept form-encoded input.`,`Every path that reaches a state change needs the same check.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`Convenience is a risk`,duration:10,caption:`Frameworks add conveniences: overrides, aliases, fallbacks. Each one is a new way to reach your code.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Bypasses`,title:`Method override and content-type confusion`,subtitle:`Conveniences that reopen doors.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`Method override`,duration:13,caption:`The X-HTTP-Method-Override header lets a client send POST but have the server treat it as PUT or DELETE.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`override`,lang:`http`,code:`POST /api/users/42 HTTP/1.1
X-HTTP-Method-Override: DELETE

// server treats this as a DELETE`}},{id:`s3`,title:`The gate mismatch`,duration:13,caption:`If CSRF checks run only for DELETE and not for POST, an attacker sends a POST with the override and slips through.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[e,n,r],packets:[{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`POST + override`,at:.2,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`treated as DELETE`,at:.6,tone:`cookie`},{from:`target`,to:`browser`,label:`CSRF check skipped`,at:.85,tone:`blocked`}],note:`The check and the action disagreed on the verb.`}},{id:`s4`,title:`Content-type confusion`,duration:13,caption:`Some endpoints parse form-encoded data even though they expect JSON. A plain cross-site form suddenly reaches them.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`unexpected input`,lang:`http`,code:`POST /api/email HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

email=attacker@evil.example

→ parsed as JSON anyway`}},{id:`s5`,title:`The GET fallback`,duration:12,caption:`A few frameworks accept a query parameter like _method=PUT, letting a GET trigger an update. Now an image tag is enough again.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`GET becomes PUT`,lang:`html`,code:`<img src="https://api.example/email
     ?_method=PUT&email=attacker@evil.example">`}},{id:`s5b`,title:`Why it is sneaky`,duration:12,caption:`The developer checked the main route. The override, the fallback, and the alias each reach the same code by a path nobody guarded.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Many roads, one check`,items:[`Override headers and _method params.`,`Form-encoded fallbacks on JSON routes.`,`Aliased or legacy endpoints.`]}},{id:`s6`,title:`Result`,duration:11,caption:`A state change happened through a route the team believed was safe, because the verb the user sent was not the verb the server ran.`,visual:{kind:`impact`,heading:`Gate bypassed`,before:`“we check DELETE”`,after:`a POST that became a DELETE`,note:`Same action, different check.`}},{id:`s7`,title:`The takeaway`,duration:11,caption:`Apply CSRF protection at every entry point, and treat any request that can change state as unsafe — whatever verb it claims.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Remember`,items:[`One CSRF policy for all verbs and routes.`,`Reject unknown content types and overrides.`,`Protect the action, not the verb.`]}}]}],c=[{id:`account-takeover-chain`,title:`Case study: email change to takeover`,subtitle:`How a small CSRF becomes a full account takeover.`,category:`Real world`,level:`intermediate`,accent:`danger`,summary:`A single forged request rarely ends the story. Watch how changing an email address enables a password reset, and a password reset becomes complete account takeover.`,keyPoints:[`Email change is the key that unlocks password reset.`,`Attackers chain small state changes into full compromise.`,`The most dangerous endpoints look mundane.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`The small request`,duration:10,caption:`It starts with something boring: a form that changes the email on your account. No money, no admin panel.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Real world`,title:`From email change to takeover`,subtitle:`One small CSRF, chained.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`Step one: change the email`,duration:13,caption:`The attacker uses a CSRF bug to set the account email to their own. The victim is still signed in and notices nothing.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[e,n,r],packets:[{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`auto-submit form`,at:.15,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`POST /account/email + cookie`,at:.55,tone:`cookie`}],note:`email → attacker@evil.example`}},{id:`s3`,title:`Step two: trigger a reset`,duration:12,caption:`Now the attacker visits the password reset page and enters their own email — the one the account now uses.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`password reset`,lang:`http`,code:`POST /account/reset HTTP/1.1
Host: vault.example

email=attacker@evil.example
→ reset link sent to the attacker`}},{id:`s4`,title:`Step three: reset is legitimate`,duration:13,caption:`The reset link arrives in the attacker’s inbox, so it is a perfectly valid request. There is no forgery here at all.`,visual:{kind:`browser`,url:`https://mail.example/inbox`,title:`attacker’s inbox`,lines:[{type:`text`,value:`Reset your password`},{type:`note`,value:`this link is genuinely valid`}]}},{id:`s4b`,title:`Step three b: set a password`,duration:12,caption:`The attacker sets a new password. The account email already points at them, so the reset flow accepts them as the owner.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`The chain`,items:[`1. Forge the email change.`,`2. Request a reset to that email.`,`3. Set a new password.`]}},{id:`s5`,title:`Step four: total control`,duration:12,caption:`The victim’s password still works for a while, but the attacker now owns the reset path. The account is gone.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[e,r],packets:[{from:`attacker`,to:`target`,label:`login with new password`,at:.4,tone:`forged`}],note:`The victim is locked out.`}},{id:`s5b`,title:`Why it worked`,duration:12,caption:`Each step was small and legitimate-looking. Only the first one was a CSRF bug, and it opened the rest.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`The single root flaw`,items:[`Email change had no CSRF token.`,`Password reset trusted the stored email.`,`No notification warned the victim.`]}},{id:`s6`,title:`The damage`,duration:11,caption:`From one forged form, the attacker gained the whole account and any access tied to it.`,visual:{kind:`impact`,heading:`Chained to takeover`,before:`one forged email change`,after:`complete account takeover`,note:`Small bug, total loss.`}},{id:`s7`,title:`The takeaway`,duration:12,caption:`Protect the mundane endpoints hardest. Require your password or a token for email changes, and notify the owner of every change.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Remember`,items:[`Email change is a sensitive action.`,`Require re-auth or a token for it.`,`Alert the account owner on changes.`]}}]},{id:`csrf-in-spas`,title:`CSRF in single-page apps`,subtitle:`Tokens without a form to hide them in.`,category:`Real world`,level:`intermediate`,accent:`steel`,summary:`Single-page apps talk to APIs with JavaScript, so there is no server-rendered form to carry a hidden token. The defences still work, but the plumbing looks different.`,keyPoints:[`The token usually lives in a readable cookie or a bootstrap call.`,`Send it in a custom header so it also triggers a preflight.`,`Never store the token where another origin can read it.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`No forms to hide in`,duration:11,caption:`A single-page app fetches data with JavaScript and sends JSON. There is no server-rendered form, so where does the token go?`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Real world`,title:`CSRF in single-page apps`,subtitle:`Tokens without a hidden field.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`Two common patterns`,duration:13,caption:`Either the server sends the token in a JSON bootstrap response, or it sets a non-HttpOnly cookie the JavaScript can read.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Getting the token`,items:[`A /csrf endpoint the app calls on load.`,`A readable cookie set by the server.`,`Embedded in the initial HTML as JSON.`]}},{id:`s3`,title:`Send it in a header`,duration:13,caption:`The app copies the token into a custom header on every write. That header also forces a CORS preflight the attacker cannot pass.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`the request`,lang:`javascript`,code:`fetch("/api/email", {
  method: "POST",
  credentials: "include",
  headers: {
    "Content-Type": "application/json",
    "X-CSRF-Token": getCsrfToken()
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({ email })
})`}},{id:`s3b`,title:`Double duty`,duration:12,caption:`Because it is a custom header, the request is no longer simple and the browser requires a CORS preflight first.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Two defences at once`,items:[`The token proves intent.`,`The custom header forces a preflight.`,`Strict CORS blocks foreign origins.`]}},{id:`s4`,title:`The trap: local storage`,duration:13,caption:`Do not keep the token somewhere an injected script can read. If XSS runs in your app, it can read a cookie or storage — so prevent XSS first.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Where NOT to keep it`,items:[`Do not trust local storage to be safe from XSS.`,`A readable cookie is fine only with __Host- and SameSite.`,`Always pair with strong output encoding.`]}},{id:`s5`,title:`Cookie vs header sessions`,duration:12,caption:`If your app authenticates with an Authorization header instead of cookies, CSRF largely disappears — but beware storing that token insecurely.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[n,r],packets:[{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`GET /api/me`,at:.3,tone:`request`}],note:`No cookie → nothing for a cross-site request to steal.`}},{id:`s5b`,title:`But the trade-off`,duration:12,caption:`Header-based auth moves the risk to token storage. A token in local storage is readable by any XSS, so the danger shifts rather than vanishes.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`The trade-off`,items:[`No cookie: little CSRF risk.`,`But tokens in JS storage are XSS targets.`,`Prefer short-lived tokens and strong CSP.`]}},{id:`s6`,title:`The takeaway`,duration:11,caption:`SPAs need the same proof of intent. Send a token in a custom header, keep CORS strict, and never let the attacker read it.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Remember`,items:[`Put the token in a header, not just a cookie.`,`A custom header forces a preflight.`,`Defend XSS as you would for any app.`]}}]},{id:`oauth-state`,title:`CSRF in OAuth: the state parameter`,subtitle:`Why login flows need a token too.`,category:`Real world`,level:`advanced`,accent:`danger`,summary:`OAuth’s authorization code flow is a state change, so it is CSRF-able without the state parameter. Attackers use it to silently link their account to your login.`,keyPoints:[`The authorization request itself needs CSRF protection.`,`state ties the callback to the user who started the flow.`,`Without state, an attacker can bind their account to your session.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`Login flows are state changes`,duration:11,caption:`OAuth quietly signs you in and links accounts. Anything that changes login state can be attacked with CSRF.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Real world`,title:`CSRF in OAuth`,subtitle:`The state parameter matters.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`The normal flow`,duration:13,caption:`You click “Sign in with X”. Your browser goes to the provider, then returns to the app with a code in the URL.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[t,n,r],packets:[{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`authorize?client_id=…`,at:.2,tone:`request`},{from:`target`,to:`browser`,label:`redirect ?code=abc`,at:.6,tone:`good`}],note:`The code is exchanged for a token.`}},{id:`s3`,title:`The attacker’s trick`,duration:13,caption:`The attacker starts their own OAuth flow, obtains a code for their account, and then makes the victim open the callback URL with that code.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`forced callback`,lang:`html`,code:`<img src="https://app.example/oauth/callback?code=attacker-code">`}},{id:`s4`,title:`Accounts get linked`,duration:13,caption:`The app exchanges the attacker’s code and links the attacker’s identity to the victim’s session. It looks like a normal login.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[e,n,r],packets:[{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`callback?code=attacker`,at:.4,tone:`cookie`},{from:`target`,to:`browser`,label:`account linked`,at:.8,tone:`blocked`}],note:`The attacker’s account is now bound to the victim.`}},{id:`s5`,title:`Enter state`,duration:13,caption:`The fix is a random state value the app creates before redirecting. It is stored for the session and checked when the callback returns.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`with state`,lang:`http`,code:`GET /authorize?client_id=app
   &redirect_uri=https://app.example/oauth/callback
   &state=rAnd0m-xyz`}},{id:`s5b`,title:`The mismatch`,duration:12,caption:`The attacker cannot know the victim’s state value, so their forced callback arrives without it and is rejected.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`rejected`,lang:`http`,code:`GET /oauth/callback?code=attacker-code

// expected state=rAnd0m-xyz — missing → 400`}},{id:`s6`,title:`Result without state`,duration:11,caption:`Without state, the linking succeeds and the attacker gets a foothold. With it, the flow is bound to the person who started it.`,visual:{kind:`impact`,heading:`Linked or rejected`,before:`no state → attacker account linked`,after:`state checked → callback rejected`,note:`One parameter closes it.`}},{id:`s7`,title:`The takeaway`,duration:12,caption:`Always use state in OAuth, prefer PKCE, and validate that the redirect URI is exact and registered.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Remember`,items:[`Generate and verify a random state value.`,`Use PKCE for public clients.`,`Match the redirect URI exactly.`]}}]},{id:`websocket-csrf`,title:`WebSocket CSRF`,subtitle:`The handshake the browser does by itself.`,category:`Real world`,level:`advanced`,accent:`danger`,summary:`WebSocket connections start with a normal HTTP request, and the browser will happily make it from another site — cookies and all. That makes cross-site WebSocket hijacking possible.`,keyPoints:[`The WebSocket handshake is an HTTP GET with cookies.`,`Upgrade requests are not subject to the same-origin policy.`,`Validate the Origin header during the handshake.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`A handshake behind the scenes`,duration:11,caption:`Opening a WebSocket begins with an HTTP request. And where there is an HTTP request, there can be CSRF.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Real world`,title:`WebSocket CSRF`,subtitle:`The handshake the browser makes for you.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`The upgrade request`,duration:13,caption:`The browser sends an Upgrade request to the server. It carries cookies, and the browser does not apply the same-origin policy to it.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`handshake`,lang:`http`,code:`GET /socket HTTP/1.1
Host: chat.example
Upgrade: websocket
Cookie: session=abc123
Origin: https://evil.example`}},{id:`s3`,title:`Cross-site hijacking`,duration:13,caption:`An attacker page opens a WebSocket to your server. Because the cookie rides along, the server treats it as your connection.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[e,n,r],packets:[{from:`browser`,to:`attacker`,label:`load page`,at:.06,tone:`forged`},{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`new WebSocket(…chat.example)`,at:.35,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`upgrade + cookie`,at:.7,tone:`cookie`}],note:`No SOP check on the upgrade request.`}},{id:`s4`,title:`But did the browser see it?`,duration:12,caption:`The attacker cannot read the server’s replies directly, because the response is an opaque 101. The risk depends on what messages cause.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`The subtlety`,items:[`The handshake has no SOP protection.`,`Reply contents are hard to read cross-site.`,`But the connection can still send messages.`]}},{id:`s5`,title:`When it turns dangerous`,duration:13,caption:`If the server trusts messages without checking Origin, the attacker can send commands that act as you the moment the socket opens.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`attacker script`,lang:`javascript`,code:`const ws = new WebSocket("wss://chat.example/socket")
ws.onopen = () => ws.send(
  JSON.stringify({ action: "delete", id: 42 })
)`}},{id:`s5b`,title:`The missing check`,duration:12,caption:`A server that ignores the Origin header has no way to tell your page from the attacker’s. That is the whole bug.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`The root cause`,items:[`Origin is present but ignored.`,`No token or session binding on the socket.`,`Messages treated as trusted by default.`]}},{id:`s6`,title:`Result`,duration:11,caption:`Actions run against the victim’s session over a connection they never opened. The damage depends on what the socket can do.`,visual:{kind:`impact`,heading:`Cross-site socket`,before:`a connection only you opened`,after:`a connection the attacker opened as you`,note:`Origin was the missing check.`}},{id:`s7`,title:`The takeaway`,duration:12,caption:`During the handshake, verify the Origin header, require a token or a session-bound nonce, and never trust messages just because a cookie was present.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Remember`,items:[`Validate Origin on the upgrade request.`,`Bind the socket to a session token.`,`Treat socket messages as untrusted.`]}}]},{id:`hardening-checklist`,title:`The CSRF hardening checklist`,subtitle:`Everything, in the order that matters.`,category:`Real world`,level:`intermediate`,accent:`ember`,summary:`A practical, layered recipe you can apply today: safe verbs, SameSite, tokens, Origin checks, strict CORS, and the habits that keep them working.`,keyPoints:[`Layer defences so no single failure is fatal.`,`Protect every state change, including login, logout and settings.`,`Verify, log, and never trust the client.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`Bringing it together`,duration:10,caption:`You have seen the attacks and the defences. Here is the checklist that ties them into a real policy.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Real world`,title:`The CSRF hardening checklist`,subtitle:`Layered, practical, repeatable.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`1. Safe verbs`,duration:11,caption:`Start with the design rule: GET reads, POST writes. It removes the easiest attacks before any code checks a thing.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Do first`,items:[`Never change state on GET.`,`Use POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE for writes.`,`No state changes reachable by a URL alone.`]}},{id:`s3`,title:`2. SameSite cookies`,duration:11,caption:`Set SameSite on session cookies — Lax as a default, Strict for the most sensitive. Add Secure and the __Host- prefix.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`cookie flags`,lang:`http`,code:`Set-Cookie: __Host-session=abc; Path=/; Secure;
            HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax`}},{id:`s4`,title:`3. CSRF tokens`,duration:12,caption:`Issue a strong, session-bound token and require it on every write. This is your primary, explicit check.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Tokens`,items:[`Generate with a CSPRNG, per session.`,`Verify on all state-changing requests.`,`Deliver hidden or in a custom header.`]}},{id:`s5`,title:`4. Origin checks`,duration:11,caption:`Validate the Origin header on writes, with Referer as a fallback. Reject unknown values instead of allowing them.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Origin`,items:[`Check Origin against an allow-list.`,`Reject missing or unexpected values.`,`Use it alongside, not instead of, tokens.`]}},{id:`s5b`,title:`5. Strict CORS`,duration:11,caption:`Keep CORS tight. Never reflect the Origin with credentials allowed, and prefer custom headers on APIs.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`never this`,lang:`http`,code:`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: <reflected>
Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`}},{id:`s6`,title:`Protect the boring endpoints`,duration:12,caption:`Login, logout, signup, email and password changes all need the same protection. These are the ones attackers chain.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Do not forget`,items:[`Login and logout.`,`Email, password and MFA changes.`,`Settings, forwarding rules and API keys.`]}},{id:`s6b`,title:`Watch the frameworks`,duration:12,caption:`Disable method overrides and content-type fallbacks unless you truly need them, and guard every route that ends in a write.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Framework hygiene`,items:[`Turn off _method and override headers.`,`Reject unexpected content types.`,`One CSRF policy across every route.`]}},{id:`s7`,title:`And keep XSS out`,duration:12,caption:`None of this survives an XSS bug. Encode output, use a Content-Security-Policy, and treat script injection as the top priority.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Because XSS defeats everything`,items:[`Context-aware output encoding.`,`A strict Content-Security-Policy.`,`As you fix CSRF, do not forget XSS.`]}},{id:`s8`,title:`The closing rule`,duration:12,caption:`Layer it all, protect every state change, and prove intent on the server every time. That is CSRF, handled properly.`,visual:{kind:`impact`,heading:`Defence in depth`,before:`cookies prove identity`,after:`tokens and Origin prove intent`,note:`Layers, not a single lock.`}}]}],l={id:`attacker`,label:`Attacker site`,sub:`evil.example`,x:15,y:22,tone:`attacker`},u={id:`victim`,label:`Victim`,sub:`signed in`,x:15,y:80,tone:`victim`},d={id:`browser`,label:`Victim's browser`,sub:`trusts cookies`,x:50,y:51,tone:`browser`},f={id:`target`,label:`Target site`,sub:`vault.example`,x:85,y:51,tone:`target`},p=[{id:`what-is-csrf`,title:`What CSRF actually is`,subtitle:`A forged request, sent by the victim’s own browser.`,category:`Foundations`,level:`beginner`,accent:`ember`,summary:`In six animated minutes, see why the browser’s automatic trust in cookies is the whole game.`,keyPoints:[`CSRF abuses the browser’s automatic cookie attachment.`,`The attacker never reads the response — they only cause a side effect.`,`The victim never clicks the malicious request directly.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`Cold open`,duration:6,caption:`Cross-Site Request Forgery makes a victim’s browser send a request the victim never intended.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Tutorial 01`,title:`Cross-Site Request Forgery`,subtitle:`Forge the request. Break the trust.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`The core idea`,duration:9,caption:`A website trusts requests that carry a valid session cookie — no matter who or what caused them.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`The problem in one line`,items:[`Cookies are attached automatically by the browser.`,`The server can’t tell a real click from a forged request.`,`So an attacker just needs the victim to load their page.`]}},{id:`s3`,title:`The cast`,duration:10,caption:`Four actors: the victim, their browser, the attacker’s page, and the target site.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[l,u,d,f],packets:[{from:`victim`,to:`browser`,label:`opens a page`,at:.05,tone:`request`},{from:`browser`,to:`attacker`,label:`loads attacker page`,at:.3,tone:`forged`}],note:`The browser is the confused deputy: it obeys both sites at once.`}},{id:`s4`,title:`The forged request`,duration:12,caption:`The attacker’s page silently triggers a request to the target. The browser adds the session cookie automatically.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[l,u,d,f],packets:[{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`hidden form / img`,at:.1,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`POST /account/email`,at:.45,tone:`request`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`+ session cookie`,at:.62,tone:`cookie`}],note:`SameSite=None → the cookie rides along cross-site.`}},{id:`s5`,title:`The payload`,duration:9,caption:`The entire attack can be a hidden form the attacker auto-submits when the page loads.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`attacker.html`,lang:`html`,code:`<form action="https://vault.example/account/email"
      method="POST">
  <input type="hidden" name="email"
         value="attacker@evil.example">
</form>
<script>document.forms[0].submit()<\/script>`}},{id:`s6`,title:`Impact`,duration:7,caption:`The victim’s account changes — they never typed a thing.`,visual:{kind:`impact`,heading:`Account email changed`,before:`amina@vault.example`,after:`attacker@evil.example`,note:`From there: password reset goes to the attacker.`}},{id:`s7`,title:`Recap`,duration:8,caption:`No token, no origin check → the request is indistinguishable from a legitimate one.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Remember`,items:[`CSRF causes a side effect — it doesn’t need to read data.`,`Automatic cookies are the lever.`,`The fix is to prove the request came from your own site.`]}}]},{id:`samesite-story`,title:`The SameSite story`,subtitle:`How one cookie attribute quietly defuses most CSRF.`,category:`SameSite`,level:`beginner`,accent:`steel`,summary:`Walk through Strict, Lax and None with an animated cross-site POST to see exactly when the cookie travels.`,keyPoints:[`SameSite=Strict: never cross-site.`,`SameSite=Lax: only top-level GET navigations.`,`SameSite=None: always (and requires Secure).`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`Cold open`,duration:5,caption:`SameSite decides whether your cookie crosses site boundaries.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Tutorial 02`,title:`The SameSite story`,subtitle:`One attribute, three behaviours.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`What counts as cross-site`,duration:9,caption:`Any request from a different registrable domain is cross-site — including subresources and form posts.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Cross-site means…`,items:[`Another registrable domain (evil.example vs vault.example).`,`A form post, an image, an iframe, or a fetch.`,`The browser decides, using the cookie’s SameSite value.`]}},{id:`s3`,title:`None`,duration:10,caption:`SameSite=None: the browser attaches the cookie to a cross-site POST. Wide open.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[l,u,d,f],packets:[{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`form POST`,at:.1,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`cookie SENT`,at:.5,tone:`cookie`}]}},{id:`s4`,title:`Lax`,duration:12,caption:`SameSite=Lax: a cross-site POST is blocked, but a top-level GET navigation still carries the cookie.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[l,u,d,f],packets:[{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`form POST`,at:.1,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`cookie withheld`,at:.5,tone:`blocked`},{from:`victim`,to:`browser`,label:`clicks a link (GET)`,at:.68,tone:`request`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`cookie SENT`,at:.84,tone:`cookie`}]}},{id:`s5`,title:`Strict`,duration:8,caption:`SameSite=Strict: no cross-site request is ever authenticated.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[l,u,d,f],packets:[{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`form POST`,at:.15,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`cookie withheld`,at:.55,tone:`blocked`}],note:`Powerful — but Strict can break normal navigation flows.`}},{id:`s6`,title:`Recap`,duration:8,caption:`Lax is the modern default, but it still lets GET navigations through — never change state on GET.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Rules of thumb`,items:[`Set Secure + HttpOnly + SameSite=Lax on session cookies.`,`Never let GET change state.`,`Pair SameSite with a token for state-changing routes.`]}}]},{id:`tokens-that-work`,title:`Tokens that work`,subtitle:`Synchronizer tokens, double-submit, and the ways they break.`,category:`Tokens`,level:`intermediate`,accent:`danger`,summary:`Not all anti-CSRF tokens are equal. See a session-bound token defeat an attack, then watch three weak variants fall.`,keyPoints:[`The token must be bound to the session and unpredictable.`,`Never put tokens in URLs — they leak via Referer and logs.`,`Double-submit only works when the cookie can’t be planted by the attacker.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`Cold open`,duration:5,caption:`A good token makes the forged request unauthenticated.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Tutorial 03`,title:`Tokens that work`,subtitle:`…and the ones that only look like they do.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`Synchronizer token`,duration:11,caption:`The server mints a random token into the victim’s session and expects it back exactly.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[u,d,f],packets:[{from:`target`,to:`browser`,label:`token tk_9f31…`,at:.1,tone:`good`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`action + tk_9f31…`,at:.5,tone:`good`}],note:`The attacker cannot read the token, so they cannot echo it.`}},{id:`s3`,title:`Attack fails`,duration:9,caption:`A forged request carries no token — or the wrong one — and the server rejects it outright.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[l,d,f],packets:[{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`forged POST`,at:.1,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`no valid token`,at:.55,tone:`blocked`}]}},{id:`s4`,title:`Weak #1 — unbound`,duration:10,caption:`If the server accepts any valid token from a shared pool, the attacker brings their own.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[l,d,f],packets:[{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`own valid token`,at:.12,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`token accepted!`,at:.58,tone:`forged`}],note:`Tokens must be tied to the session, not to the site.`}},{id:`s5`,title:`Weak #2 — leaked`,duration:10,caption:`A token in the URL leaks through Referer, history and logs — and the defense collapses.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`leaky URL`,lang:`text`,code:`https://docuhub.example/me?csrf_token=tk_5b28d1f09c

→ Referer header to any third party
→ Browser history
→ Server access logs`}},{id:`s6`,title:`Weak #3 — double-submit`,duration:11,caption:`Double-submit compares a cookie to a body value. If the cookie value is fixed or plantable, it fails.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[l,d,f],packets:[{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`plant cookie + body`,at:.12,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`pair matches → accepted`,at:.6,tone:`forged`}]}},{id:`s7`,title:`Recap`,duration:8,caption:`Random per-session secrets, out of URLs, bound to the session — that’s the recipe.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`A token is only as strong as`,items:[`Randomness (unguessable).`,`Binding (tied to this user’s session).`,`Secrecy (never in URLs, never in logs).`]}}]},{id:`anatomy-forged-request`,title:`Anatomy of a forged request`,subtitle:`Follow one request, hop by hop, from lured click to changed state.`,category:`Foundations`,level:`intermediate`,accent:`ember`,summary:`A frame-by-frame trace of a real CSRF: the attacker page, the auto-submit, the cookie, the server’s blind trust.`,keyPoints:[`The victim only has to load (or click) the attacker’s page.`,`The request looks identical to a legitimate one.`,`Defenses inspect the request’s provenance, not its appearance.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`Cold open`,duration:5,caption:`One request. Five hops. Zero user intent.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Tutorial 04`,title:`Anatomy of a forged request`,subtitle:`A frame-by-frame trace.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`Hop 1 — the lure`,duration:8,caption:`The victim opens the attacker’s page — anything from a forum post to a malvertising redirect.`,visual:{kind:`browser`,url:`https://evil.example/win-a-prize`,title:`Congratulations, you won!`,lines:[{type:`text`,value:`Claim your prize now…`},{type:`note`,value:`<!-- the page hides a form -->`}]}},{id:`s3`,title:`Hop 2 — the auto-submit`,duration:10,caption:`A hidden form submits itself the moment the page parses. No click required.`,visual:{kind:`browser`,url:`https://evil.example/win-a-prize`,title:`Rendering…`,lines:[{type:`hidden`,label:`email`,value:`attacker@evil.example`},{type:`button`,label:`Submitting…`,danger:!0},{type:`flash`,value:`Request fired to vault.example`}]}},{id:`s4`,title:`Hop 3 — the cookie`,duration:10,caption:`The browser attaches the vault.example session cookie — it doesn’t know this request was forged.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[d,f],packets:[{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`POST /account/email`,at:.12,tone:`request`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`Cookie: session=…`,at:.42,tone:`cookie`}],note:`SameSite=None is what makes this possible.`}},{id:`s5`,title:`Hop 4 — blind trust`,duration:9,caption:`The server sees a valid session and performs the action. It never asks “did the user mean this?”`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`What the server checks`,items:[`Is there a valid session cookie? ✅`,`Did the user intend this? ❓ — never asked`]}},{id:`s6`,title:`Hop 5 — impact`,duration:7,caption:`State changes. The attacker is now in control of a recovery channel.`,visual:{kind:`impact`,heading:`Email changed`,before:`marco@onestore.example`,after:`attacker@evil.example`,note:`Next: password reset lands in the attacker’s inbox.`}}]},{id:`defending-an-api`,title:`Defending an API`,subtitle:`Origin checks, required headers, and defense in depth.`,category:`Defense`,level:`advanced`,accent:`steel`,summary:`Layer the modern defenses: validate Origin, require a custom header, bind a token, and set SameSite correctly.`,keyPoints:[`Origin/Referer can’t be forged by a web page.`,`A required custom header forces a CORS preflight.`,`Layer SameSite + token + Origin for defense in depth.`],scenes:[{id:`s1`,title:`Cold open`,duration:5,caption:`Strong defense isn’t one control — it’s several that cover each other.`,visual:{kind:`title`,badge:`Tutorial 05`,title:`Defending an API`,subtitle:`Origin, headers, tokens, SameSite.`}},{id:`s2`,title:`Control 1 — Origin`,duration:10,caption:`The browser sets Origin for the real request. An attacker page can only advertise its own origin.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[l,d,f],packets:[{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`forged POST`,at:.12,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`Origin: evil.example`,at:.55,tone:`blocked`}],note:`Server: expected Origin mismatch → reject.`}},{id:`s3`,title:`Control 2 — custom header`,duration:10,caption:`Require X-Requested-With. A cross-site HTML request can’t set it, and fetch triggers a preflight the server denies.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`server expectation`,lang:`http`,code:`POST /api/keys/revoke HTTP/1.1
Origin: https://clouddesk.example
X-Requested-With: XMLHttpRequest   ← required
Cookie: session=…`}},{id:`s4`,title:`Control 3 — session token`,duration:9,caption:`A random token, stored in the session and echoed in a header or hidden field, defeats the forgery outright.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Token hygiene`,items:[`Random per session — never a constant.`,`Delivered in a header or hidden field, not the URL.`,`Compared against the session’s stored value.`]}},{id:`s5`,title:`Control 4 — SameSite`,duration:9,caption:`Mark session cookies Secure + HttpOnly + SameSite=Lax as a cheap, browser-enforced baseline.`,visual:{kind:`code`,title:`Set-Cookie`,lang:`http`,code:`Set-Cookie: session=…; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax; Path=/`}},{id:`s6`,title:`Defense in depth`,duration:9,caption:`Any one control can have a gap. Together they make the forged request fail at multiple layers.`,visual:{kind:`flow`,nodes:[l,d,f],packets:[{from:`attacker`,to:`browser`,label:`forged POST`,at:.12,tone:`forged`},{from:`browser`,to:`target`,label:`blocked ×4`,at:.5,tone:`blocked`}],note:`SameSite + token + Origin + header requirement.`}},{id:`s7`,title:`Recap`,duration:7,caption:`You now have the full picture: how the forgery works, and how to make it impossible.`,visual:{kind:`bullets`,heading:`Your checklist`,items:[`Reads on GET, writes on POST.`,`Session-bound CSRF token on every write.`,`Validate Origin/Referer.`,`Secure + HttpOnly + SameSite cookies.`]}}]},...i,...a,...o,...s,...c],m=Object.fromEntries(p.map(e=>[e.id,e]));export{m as n,p as t};